WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:16.750 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  for a game that was long in development  as a cooperative horde based shooter the  conspicuous and relatively quick edition  of battle royale in fortnight seemed to  be a mote act on to capitalize on a  trend however it's simple building  system in loose shooting mechanics not  only set it apart from other games built  on the same premise but work extremely  well to make a uniquely chaotic and  surprisingly deep deathmatch everything  about for tonight's presentation admits  a light-hearted nature you start a match  by jumping out of a party bus held up by  balloons that flies across the massive  map weapons ammo and health items litter  the silly name cities all using  alliteration tomato town moisty mire  tilted towers just the name a few even  enemies don't really die there  teleported after getting knocked out but  don't let that first impression fool you  the further you get into a match the  more you see how for tonight's gameplay  elements have to be used in clever and  complex ways unique to fortnight is a  streamlined building system thanks to a  snappy elegant grid-based layout and the  intuitive control scheme getting the  hang of building isn't much of a hurdle  at first glance it's as if the original  save the world mode had its mechanics  haphazardly dropped into the hundred  player last person standing premise but  this is the foundation that makes for a  myriad of tactical possibilities like  improvising a sky-high staircase to  climb a mountain to get to higher ground  or swiftly creating your own cover as  you run across an open field and closing  on opponents literally bridging the gap  between mountains can turn long-range  shootouts into close quarter brawls it's  a dynamic system that always gives you  an opportunity to improvise even when  you think your back is against the wall  for example players will often protect  themselves with structures that act as  makeshift bunkers to undercut that you  can put pressure on them by constructing  your own path into their territory and  these moments the intrinsically  rewarding nature of fortnight's shines  through conflict isn't just about  landing a precise shot or spotting an  enemy first quick wit and improvisation  with the given toolset put you in a  position to create your own path to  success eliminations and victories feel  very much earned especially because a  late game often consists of which  players squad has the best architectural  acumen into ever-changing safe zones  approaches to combat also rely on the  weapons you can scavenge a typical  Arsenal made up of rifles submachine  guns shotguns and pistols have  color-coded tiers to indicate varying  levels of power and rarity each gun has  a sensible use case however traps and  Explosives mix things up a bit this is  another aspect in which fortnight  divergence for many other battle royale  games shooting is fast and loose akin to  an arena shooter mid-range firefights in  close quarters combat feel more like a  fatal dance in and around the structures  plopped into the environment for night  isn't a tactical shooter in the  traditional sense but offers its own  bevy of strategic options to keep  players on their toes enemy engagement  still carries the risk you'd expect from  games of this ilk by nature of having  one life to live per match and the  relatively quick time to kill even after  downing a chug-chug 4 full shield  well-placed shots from a legendary or  epic will make short work of anyone  however the brisk pace at which matches  moved trades unnerving tension for a  higher frequency of action yet as with  any Battle Royale game looting for  resources sits at the core of matches  and eats up much of your time the system  in place for loot and resource gathering  is efficient but it grows tiresome after  consecutive matches as swinging the  pickaxe at trees and houses for  necessary materials grows increasingly  repetitive another area in which 4/9 is  a bit thin is in its map design a  shortcoming that's twofold the sprawling  load map features a variety of cute  thematic areas but there's a feeling  that many of the maps landmarks lexa  fists occation in physical layouts and  in density of loot placement to its  credit the maps of verticality brings  out the best in your construction  abilities but city centers like tomato  town have little to work with when two  squads land in the area a slightly more  intricate town like snobby shores is  sometimes devoid of useful items it'd be  easier to overlook this if you didn't  have to trek across to a nearby town on  foot that's likely to have already been  looted but such is the case  now in just about half a year epic  proved its supporting for tonight  continually with new content those who  have been playing the game are aware of  the limited time modes that put a slight  twist on the standard mode snipers only  an explosives only matches have added a  neat touch but past modes like 50 V 50  and teams of 20 do much more to change  fortnight's pace and open up new ways to  play the game if that's any indication  fortnight could have plenty more to  offer as it evolves further this is a  free to play game so you should be aware  that it heavily relies on  microtransactions to sustain itself a  ten-dollar battle pass opens up a slew  of skins to earn and provides new goals  to work towards it's a reasonable system  and that these objectives reward you  with cosmetic items that visibly pop  within fortnight's bright art style  there's nothing to infringe on how the  game plays thankfully if you wish to  engage in making your pickaxe look like  a toy or Don's seasonal outfits or get  the latest viral dance as an emote you  either have to put in the time to earn  it or shell out money for the game's  in-game currency called V bugs  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  while there are several parts in the  games ecosystem for Knights biggest  accomplishment is in how it seamlessly  merges a number of simple mechanics to  create a distinguishable Battle Royale  game what looks to be a straightforward  building system that elite escalates to  an elaborate display of tactical prowess  as the saying goes it's easy to learn  hard to master although a few  shortcomings in the map design  eventually surface and fatigue and  looting can set in fortnight rarely  fails at challenging you in unexpected  ways resulting in something more than  just another typical last person  standing shooter  [Music]

